## Rendering path, and why it is fast (12 October 2026)

`render_glsl_batch` cooks one HyperFrames project (raw WebGL2, one fullscreen triangle, `data-no-timeline`, `hf-seek` listener and `__hf.buildReady` as in `templates/device3d/scene.js`); each case is shown at time `i / fps` and all cases are captured by a single `hyperframes snapshot --at ...` run, so the browser launches once. A shader that fails to compile paints magenta, so a broken block turns red instead of passing on a stale frame. PNG snapshots were chosen over an MP4 render because H.264 4:2:0 would add compression error to a comparison measured in single levels. Measured on this Mac: 6 cases at 256 x 256 in 5.1 s, one 1920 x 1080 frame in 2.9 s, color pipeline exact (a uv gradient test matched `round(uv * 255)` with 0 error, no colour management on the canvas). (12 October 2026)

